    },
    {
     "kind": "read",
     "step": "read",
     "title": "Anti-derivative",
     "paras": [
      "Finding f(x) from f′(x) is anti-differentiation, or integration.",
      "The set of all anti-derivatives is the indefinite integral ∫ f(x) dx.",
      "If F′(x) = f(x), then ∫ f(x) dx = F(x) + C. C is the constant of integration."
     ],
     "ref": "Definition 2.14, p.136",
     "id": "g12.maths.u2.s2-3-2.11"
    },
    {
     "kind": "read",
     "step": "read",
     "title": "Power rule for integrals",
     "paras": [
      "∫ x^α dx = x^(α+1)/(α + 1) + C, for α ≠ −1.",
      "∫ k dx = kx + C.",
      "∫ kf dx = k∫ f dx, and ∫ (f ± g) dx = ∫ f dx ± ∫ g dx."
     ],
     "note": "Raise the power by 1, divide by the new power.",
     "ref": "p.136–137",
     "id": "g12.maths.u2.s2-3-2.12"
    },
